{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"## Context / Problem\nGit failure strings are classified at five sites with different verdicts for the same text:\n- `_store/gitutil.py:215,221,517` — index-lock and transient-FS markers, retried locally.\n- `_store/push_classify.py:48,61,110` — non-FF / policy-decline / transport markers.\n- `_engine/rebar_reconciler/_ref_lock_push.py:68,74` and `_advisory_lock.py:285` — lease/CAS mismatch, terminal.\n- `_store/event_append.py:465` — invalid-object recovery.\n\n`cannot lock ref` receives three different verdicts. The synthetic rc-124 `git timed out after` result is built by FOUR places — `push.py:71 _git`, `sync.py:66 _git`, `event_commit_git.py:64 _run_git`, and `gitutil.py:604` (`run_git_write._bounded_once`) — and treated as retriable by only one classifier. `wrongful-chemic-squeaker` added the transient retry to `s3_doctor` only, leaving `sync._union_merge` and `push_recovery` merges without it.\n\n## Approach\nAdd `src/rebar/_store/git_outcome.py`: one marker registry and `classify(result, *, operation) -> GitOutcome` with kinds `LOCK, TRANSIENT_FS, TRANSPORT, NON_FF, CAS_MISMATCH, POLICY_DECLINE, DIRTY_WD, UNMERGED, INVALID_OBJECT, FATAL` (ten), the subtractive \"policy wins over non-FF\" rule applied once. Existing predicates become lookups; marker tables move, not change.\n\nSeparately, `gitutil.py` gains `run_git_bounded(...)` — the ONE construction of the synthetic rc-124 timeout result — and the three shims plus `run_git_write._bounded_once` delegate to it.\n\nTwo owners, because these are two constructs: `git_outcome.py` owns the marker STRINGS; `gitutil.py` owns the synthetic-timeout CONSTRUCTION (that is where the shared runner already lives; moving `run_git` itself is out of scope).\n\n## Guard construction\nPer the parent epic `airborne-wellloved-kingfisher`: owner exempt, match the AST shape named here, `# <construct>-ok: <reason>` escape with a mandatory reason, behaviour pinned only by the named existing tests.\n\nGuard A (markers) — over `src/rebar/_store/*.py` and `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/*.py`, owner `git_outcome.py` exempt: no non-docstring string LITERAL (AST `Constant`, so comment/docstring prose is ignored) may contain `index.lock`, `non-fast-forward`, `cannot lock ref`, or `could not parse head`. Escape: `# git-marker-ok: <reason>`. Two escapes are legitimate — `gitutil._LOCK_REF_RE` parses the ref name out of the exhaustion message, and `_reclaim_if_stale_index_lock` joins the lock file's PATH; neither is a verdict.\n\nGuard B (timeout) — over `src/rebar/_store/*.py`, owner `gitutil.py` exempt: no `subprocess.CompletedProcess` call may carry BOTH the int literal `124` AND a string/f-string argument containing `timed out after`. That conjunction is what distinguishes a construction from the `\"git timed out after\"` marker row, which stays a legitimate table entry. Escape: `# git-timeout-ok: <reason>`.\n\n## Constraint: the classifier signature\n`classify(result, *, operation) -> GitOutcome`, where `result` carries the FULL git outcome — command args, returncode, stdout, stderr — not just stderr text. Two load-bearing reasons:\n\n1. **A marker alone is ambiguous.** The same string means a retriable local lock, a non-CAS push reject, or a held advisory lock depending on the operation. The registry is keyed `(marker, operation)`, not `marker`.\n2. **Some verdicts are not marker-based at all.** `_advisory_lock._is_cas_mismatch` discriminates on command shape and exit code: `\"update-ref\" in args and ref_name in args`, then `returncode == 128` OR (`returncode == 1` and `\"cannot lock ref\" in stderr`). A `(marker, operation) -> kind` TABLE cannot express that. So the registry admits two entry kinds: a **marker row** (common case) and a **structural predicate** registered for an operation, receiving the whole outcome object. `_is_cas_mismatch` MOVES into the registry as the `ref-cas` predicate **verbatim** — logic unchanged, still taking the `subprocess.CalledProcessError` it takes today (`.cmd`, `.returncode`, `.stderr`) — and `_advisory_lock` re-exports it under the same name so `_ref_lock`'s `acquire/release/steal/renew` seams are untouched.\n\nThis section is authoritative over any earlier `classify(result)` wording.\n\n## Constraint: `cannot lock ref` MUST NOT be merged\nIt carries three deliberately different, bug-hardened verdicts:\n- `gitutil._is_git_lock_error` — a LOCAL ref-lock conflict, ridden out (retriable).\n- `_ref_lock_push.py:68` — in `PUSH_REJECT_MARKERS` but explicitly NOT a lease mismatch: **bug 4afc** established that only `stale info` is the `--force-with-lease` signal, since `cannot lock ref` also covers hook declines, rate limits, server errors and remote ref.lock contention. **Bug ebee** added `fatal error in commit_refs` to the same non-CAS set.\n- `_advisory_lock.py:305` — with exit 128 or exit 1, means the advisory lock is HELD (terminal).\n\nCollapsing these would re-open 4afc and ebee. The divergence is PRESERVED; the guard proves the marker strings live in one module, not that they carry one verdict.\n\n## Constraint: the tenth kind, INVALID_OBJECT\n`event_append.py:465` `_INVALID_OBJECT_MARKERS = (\"invalid object\", \"error building trees\")`, consumed by `_is_invalid_object_error`, is git `write-tree` refusing a commit because an index entry references an object MISSING from the object DB. Its handler is a **recovery** (rebuild the index and retry), not a terminal failure. Folding it into `FATAL` would delete that recovery, so the registry carries a tenth kind, `INVALID_OBJECT`, and `_is_invalid_object_error` becomes a lookup against it like the other predicates.\n\n## Scope\n`src/rebar/_store/`: `git_outcome.py` (new), `gitutil.py`, `push_classify.py`, `push.py`, `sync.py`, `push_recovery.py`, `event_commit_git.py`, `event_append.py`, `s3_doctor.py`; `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/`: `_ref_lock_push.py`, `_advisory_lock.py`; `tests/unit/store/test_git_outcome.py` (new).\n\n## Testing\nTable-driven test: one captured stderr per kind maps to exactly one kind. Edge: a stderr carrying both `rejected` and a policy-decline marker classifies POLICY_DECLINE. Pinning: `tests/unit/test_gitutil_transient_write_retry.py`, `tests/unit/test_lock_retry_zebrafish.py`, `tests/unit/test_lock_stale_reclaim.py`, `tests/unit/test_push_rejection_surfacing.py`, `tests/unit/test_push_policy.py`, `tests/integration/test_s3_doctor*.py`, and `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/state/test_ref_lock*.py` / `test_advisory_lock*.py` pass unchanged.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- [ ] Every current marker string resolves to one kind for a given operation, with each caller's kind-to-action mapping unchanged; checked by the table test plus the pinning suites.\n- [ ] `sync._union_merge` and `push_recovery`'s merges retry TRANSIENT_FS faults through the shared transient-fault retry; checked by a new test injecting `could not parse HEAD` once into each and asserting the merge then succeeds.\n- [ ] Guard A passes: the four marker strings appear as string literals only in `git_outcome.py`, modulo escapes carrying a reason.\n- [ ] Guard B passes: zero synthetic-timeout constructions in `src/rebar/_store` outside `gitutil.py`, and exactly one inside it; the three shims are deleted.\n- [ ] `cannot lock ref` keeps its three distinct verdicts; checked by a table test asserting each operation's verdict for that one string.\n- [ ] `_is_cas_mismatch`'s exit-code + command-shape discrimination is preserved exactly (exit 128, or exit 1 with `cannot lock ref`, only for an `update-ref` naming the ref); checked by a test driving all four combinations through the registry.\n- [ ] `INVALID_OBJECT` is a distinct kind whose action remains index-rebuild-then-retry; checked by a test injecting `invalid object` into an event append and asserting the rebuild path still runs and the append then succeeds."}},"env_id":"b8a354e0-12c3-4cf2-bbf1-fcc9685d5126","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1787376344744960001,"uuid":"863fa4e3-316d-4344-b1d9-e868b1e9a5a3"}